---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import cfg from '../../../../branding.config.json';

const repoUrl = `https://github.com/${cfg.orgHandle}/${cfg.repoName}`;
const advisoriesUrl = `${repoUrl}/security/advisories`;
---

<Site
  title="Security model"
  description="Threat boundary, sandbox tiers, capability tokens, vault, hygiene gates, and how to report a vulnerability."
>
  <div class="content">
    <h2>Security model</h2>
    <p>
      The threat boundary is simple: the renderer is hostile territory.
      Web content is never trusted; every privileged action crosses a
      capability-checked gate with a human approval where it matters.
    </p>
    <h2>What enforces it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Sandboxed execution.</strong> Tier-1 confinement on all
        three platforms: Windows job objects plus an AppContainer profile
        with zero capabilities; Linux Landlock filesystem rules plus a
        seccomp-bpf deny on outbound network; macOS Seatbelt profile.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Capability tokens.</strong> Tool dispatch is minted and
        verified with HMAC-SHA256 under a device root key. No ambient
        authority: each call carries exactly the grant it needs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>OS-keychain vault.</strong> Provider keys live only in the
        platform keychain. The local store holds non-secret operational
        data under filesystem ACLs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local-only wire protocol.</strong> The desktop shell talks
        to its core over named pipes and Unix sockets. Never TCP. No
        accounts, no telemetry, no open ports.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hygiene gates in CI.</strong> Six scanners (author
        identity, paths, metadata, secrets, names, fixtures) plus lint,
        typecheck, an explicit-type gate, and signed-commit enforcement
        run on every change.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2>
    <p>
      Use the repository's private channel so details stay out of the
      public tracker until a fix ships:
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={advisoriesUrl}>Private vulnerability reporting</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Include the version or commit, the platform, what you expected,
      and what happened instead. Screenshots of rendered output help;
      never send real credentials.
    </p>
  </div>
</Site>
